# Install as /etc/freeradius/3.0/sites-available/originate-coa, then:
#     ln -s ../sites-available/originate-coa /etc/freeradius/3.0/sites-enabled/originate-coa
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
# `update coa { ... }` in sites-available/wavly tells FreeRADIUS to SEND a
# Change-of-Authorization packet, but it does not say where to. This file is the
# where: one home_server per router, each referenced by that client's
# `coa_server` in clients.conf.
#
# Without it the server logs
#     WARNING: Unknown destination <nas>:3799 for CoA request
# and the CoA is silently dropped - so FUP throttling appears to work (no error in
# our logs, the accounting record says the cap was crossed) while the customer
# keeps downloading at full speed.
#
# WHY THE SECRET MATCHES THE CLIENT SECRET
# Upstream's example says the CoA secret "SHOULD NOT be the same as the shared
# secret in a client section". That is good general advice and we deliberately do
# not follow it, because RouterOS has exactly ONE secret per RADIUS client entry
# (/radius add ... secret=...) and `/radius incoming` authenticates CoA and
# Disconnect packets with that same secret. There is no way to configure a second
# one. So on MikroTik the two must match or every CoA is rejected.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS ENOUGH TO HAVE ITS OWN FILE
# CoA is the only mechanism that can act on a customer who is ALREADY online.
# Without it: a subscription that expires at 23:59 keeps working until the device
# happens to re-authenticate (potentially days later); a customer who blows
# through their data cap stays at full speed until the same thing happens; and
# Simultaneous-Use cannot actually drop the extra session. All three are the
# difference between a business that enforces its terms and one that hopes.
#
# And CoA cannot be originated on a wall clock - FreeRADIUS can only send it while
# handling an incoming Access-Request or Accounting-Request. So the expiry case is
# driven by syncd calling bin/wv-coa, which sends the same packet type to the same
# port. Both paths are configured here and in the router's firewall.
# =============================================================================

